              "The classic formalization is the Byzantine Generals problem (Lamport, Shostak and Pease, 1982). Several divisions of an army camp around a city, each led by a general, must agree on a common plan: attack or retreat. They communicate only by messenger, and some of the generals may be traitors who send conflicting messages deliberately. The requirement is Byzantine agreement: all loyal generals adopt the same plan, and if the commander is loyal, that plan is exactly the one the commander issued. The proven result is that agreement is achievable if and only if more than two-thirds of the generals are loyal — that is, with n total nodes the system tolerates at most f = (n - 1) / 3 Byzantine faults. A worked trace: with n = 4 and f = 1 traitor, three loyal generals can still reach identical decisions because each relayed value can be cross-checked against two independent copies.",
