              "A cryptographic hash function maps an input of any size to a fixed-size digest — SHA-256 always produces 256 bits, whether the input is the word 'hello' or a gigabyte file. It is a one-way compressor: a sequence of compression rounds permutes the internal state using the message, so every input bit influences every output bit. Written h: {0,1}* -> {0,1}^256, it is computable in microseconds forward but computationally infeasible backward. Hashes are the load-bearing primitive of blockchain: block identifiers, Merkle roots, addresses, proof-of-work targets and signature commitments are all hash outputs.",
              "Four security properties define the primitive, and each one maps to a blockchain job. Pre-image resistance: given y, finding any x with H(x) = y is infeasible — this protects passwords and makes a hash of a public key a safe short address. Second pre-image resistance: given x, finding x' != x with H(x') = H(x) is infeasible — this stops an attacker swapping one transaction for another with the same Merkle leaf. Collision resistance: finding any pair x != x' with H(x) = H(x') is infeasible (birthday bound: about 2^128 work for SHA-256) — this makes the block-header hash a unique fingerprint, so 'the chain of most work' is unambiguous. Determinism plus avalanche: the same input always yields the same output, and flipping one input bit changes about half the output bits, making tampering instantly visible.",
