              "The mathematics rests on trapdoor one-way functions: easy to compute, infeasible to invert without the trapdoor information. RSA uses the difficulty of factoring the product of two large primes; the public key is (n, e) and the private key is the inverse of e modulo the totient of n. Elliptic-curve cryptography (ECC) uses the algebra of points on a curve such as secp256k1, the curve Bitcoin chose: y^2 = x^3 + 7 over a finite field, with a base point G of large prime order. The public key is the curve point Q = k * G where k is the private key scalar; recovering k from Q is the elliptic-curve discrete logarithm problem, believed infeasible. ECC delivers the security of 3072-bit RSA with a 256-bit key — smaller keys, faster operations, smaller signatures, which is why constrained blockchain nodes prefer it.",
