              "A digital signature is the asymmetric-key analogue of a handwritten signature, with strictly stronger guarantees. The signer computes sig = Sign(sk, message) using the private key; anyone can run Verify(pk, message, sig) -> true/false with the public key. The signature binds a specific message to a specific key, so four properties hold at once: authenticity (the message really came from the key holder), integrity (any change to the message invalidates the signature), non-repudiation (the signer cannot plausibly deny signing, since only the private key could have produced it), and unforgeability (forging a signature without the key is computationally infeasible). In blockchain the message is a transaction — inputs, outputs, amounts — so a valid signature is exactly the proof that the owner of the coins authorizes this specific transfer.",
              "Bitcoin uses ECDSA over secp256k1; Ethereum uses the same curve with a recovery trick that saves bytes. ECDSA signing: pick a fresh random nonce r each signature (never reuse — leaked or repeated r leaks the private key algebraically, which broke the PlayStation 3 signing key and drained Android Bitcoin wallets in 2013); compute the curve point R = r*G; set s = r^-1 (z + x_R * k) mod n, where z is the hash of the message and k the private key; publish (r, s) as the signature. Verification recomputes the same algebra from the public key, the message hash and (r, s). Deterministic nonce generation (RFC 6979, deriving r from the key and message) removes the fatal randomness dependency.",
