              "A block is the unit of batching and commitment: a header plus a body of transactions. Bitcoin caps body size at about 1 MB (roughly 2,000-3,000 transactions), Ethereum blocks are gas-bounded (30 million gas on mainnet post-2024, so a few hundred transactions of typical complexity). The header is the part that matters cryptographically — it is small (Bitcoin: 80 bytes), fixed-format, and it is what the proof of work commits to, what the chain links through, and what light clients download.",
              "Bitcoin's 80-byte header has exactly six fields, and each exists for a reason. Version (4 bytes): protocol/soft-fork signaling bits. Previous block hash (32 bytes): the SHA-256d of the parent header — this is the chain link. Merkle root (32 bytes): SHA-256d over the transaction tree, committing the body. Timestamp (4 bytes): Unix seconds, allowed some drift for clock skew. Bits/target (4 bytes): the compressed difficulty, i.e. what the hash must be below. Nonce (4 bytes): the free variable miners grind. Every field except the nonce is pinned; miners spin the nonce (and, when it exhausts, the extraNonce inside the coinbase, which changes the Merkle root and gives a fresh search space).",
              "The hash chain is the integrity mechanism, and it is worth tracing precisely. Header_n contains H(header_n-1). Since H is collision-resistant, changing anything in block n-1 changes its header hash, invalidating the stored link in block n; fixing block n changes its hash, invalidating block n+1, and so on to the tip. An attacker editing a transaction in block 100 must therefore re-mine block 100 and every block after it — a full redo of the accumulated proof of work — while the honest network keeps extending the real tip. This is why 'immutable' really means economically immutable: rewriting gets exponentially more expensive with depth. Six confirmations is the conventional settlement rule because by then an attacker's reorganization cost has typically outrun the value at stake.",
