              "A Merkle tree is a binary hash tree over the transaction list. Level 0: hash every transaction to a leaf, H(tx_i). Each higher level hashes pairs: parent = H(left || right); if a level has an odd node, the last one is duplicated with itself. The single surviving root goes into the block header. The construction is bottom-up, deterministic, and the root is a 32-byte commitment to every transaction in the block — change any leaf anywhere and the root changes.",
              "The killer property is the efficient membership proof. To prove transaction T is in a block, you do not need the whole block: you need T itself plus its Merkle path — the sibling hash at each level, log2(n) hashes for n leaves. Verification: hash T, XOR-append each sibling in order, and check the final value equals the header's root. A block with 1,024 transactions needs just 10 sibling hashes (320 bytes) for a proof of roughly 350 bytes total. Worked trace for 4 transactions [A, B, C, D]: leaves HA, HB, HC, HD; level 1 H01 = H(HA||HB), H23 = H(HC||HD); root = H(H01||H23). Proving C: supply HD and H01; compute H23 = H(HC||HD), then H(H01||H23), compare to root. Three hash computations instead of four full transactions — and the saving scales logarithmically.",
              "This is exactly what a Simple Payment Verification (SPV) light client exploits. An SPV wallet downloads only the 80-byte headers (about 4.2 MB per 50,000 blocks, versus hundreds of gigabytes of full ledger), follows the longest valid header chain (which carries the accumulated proof of work), and, for its own incoming payments, asks full nodes for Merkle proofs of the relevant transactions. Trust model: SPV trusts that the heaviest chain is honest at the block level but verifies transaction inclusion cryptographically — it cannot detect invalid transactions that miners accepted, only confirm which transactions were committed. That is the precise sentence examiners want for 'what can an SPV node not do'.",
