            "The 51 percent (majority) attack is the canonical threat to resource-based consensus. An attacker controlling more than half the hash power (or stake weight) can mine a private alternative chain faster than the public one, release it when long enough, and reorganize recent history — reversing their own transactions (double-spend against exchanges) while censoring new ones. They cannot steal other users' coins: signatures still bind spends to their owners, so the attack is limited to reordering, censoring and reversing. The economics: the attacker burns real mining cost to build the private chain and, once the attack is public, the coin's price typically collapses — destroying the value of any coins they hold, which is the economic self-defence even when the cryptographic threshold is crossed.",
            "Attack cost is estimable, which is why the threat is manageable in practice. For Bitcoin, renting enough hash power for an hour via nicehash-style markets or buying ASICs runs into millions of dollars per day, against a coin whose market capitalization dwarfs that; for small PoW coins, however, rentable hash power has actually enabled real attacks (ETC suffered 51 percent reorgs exceeding 3,000 blocks in 2020; Bitcoin Gold was attacked in 2018). The lesson examiners want: security scales with the absolute cost of majority power, so small networks are structurally more vulnerable — sometimes cited as the reason to merge mining or checkpoint to a larger chain.",
            "POS variants of the majority threat sharpen the analysis. With one-third of stake an attacker can stop finality (liveness attack) and with two-thirds can finalize conflicting checkpoints (safety attack) — but every provable violation is slashable, so the attack destroys the attacker's own capital, potentially billions. The residual risks are non-attributable ones: censorship (withholding attestations) and MEV-driven reorg incentives where extracted value exceeds block rewards; mitigations are proposer-builder separation, attestation deadlines and, ultimately, social recovery — the community coordinates a minority fork that excludes attackers, which is what makes POS's final failure mode governance rather than cryptography.",
            "Beyond majority control, the threat list completes the security picture. Selfish mining (Eyal-Sirer): a withholding miner strategically releases blocks to waste honest work, gaining disproportionate share above ~25-33 percent hash power — mitigations: propagate-fast relay networks, uncle counting. Finney and race attacks: covered in 2.1 as merchant-side risks. Nothing-at-stake and long-range attacks: POS-specific, covered in 2.3. Timejacking: feeding false timestamps to skew difficulty. Bribery/rental attacks: paying honest miners or renting hash power temporarily — often cheaper than buying hardware. The common mitigation is monitoring: reorg-depth alerts, pool-concentration dashboards and exchange confirmation policies scaled to deposit size.",
