            "A smart contract is a program stored on a blockchain that executes automatically when its conditions are met, with three defining properties. Self-enforcing: no intermediary interprets or applies it — the code is the agreement, executed by consensus nodes. Immutable (by default): deployed bytecode cannot be edited; a bug is permanent unless upgrade patterns were designed in. Trustless counterparty: you can transact with a stranger because enforcement is cryptographic, not institutional. Nick Szabo coined the term in the 1990s with the vending machine as the canonical example: the machine enforces the sale — money in, product out — with no clerk; smart contracts generalize the vending machine to any codifiable rule, with blockchain providing the tamper-proof execution environment Szabo's era lacked.",
