            "Deployment is a transaction like any other, with the recipient field empty. The transaction's data field carries init bytecode: solc output that first runs the constructor (reading immutable values, setting storage) and then returns the runtime bytecode, which the EVM stores at the freshly computed contract address. Address derivation: CREATE address = last 20 bytes of Keccak256(rlp(sender, nonce)) — deterministic given sender and nonce; CREATE2 (Section 3.7) lets a factory predetermine addresses from a salt, enabling counterfactual deployment. After deployment, the ABI is published so wallets and frontends can encode calls; verification services (Etherscan) recompile published source and match the on-chain bytecode so users can read what they are interacting with.",
            "Immutability meets reality: contracts need upgrades, and the proxy pattern reconciles the two. Transparent/UUPS proxy: users always interact with the proxy address; it stores all state and delegatecalls into an implementation contract; upgrading means the proxy points to new implementation code — state survives, logic swaps. The delegatecall subtlety is that storage layout must match between implementations (append-only variables, never reorder) or state is corrupted. Beacon and Diamond (multi-facet) patterns scale this to many implementations. The trade-off to state honestly: upgradeability is admin power — a pause-and-upgrade key is exactly the centralization blockchains exist to remove, so mature projects renounce admin keys after a stabilization window or route upgrades through timelocks and governance votes.",
            "Security is the discipline that dominates this domain professionally, and the taxonomy of classic vulnerabilities is a guaranteed exam section. Reentrancy: an external call hands control to attacker code which re-enters before state updates (the DAO, 2016) — defence: checks-effects-interactions ordering and reentrancy guards. Integer overflow: pre-0.8 wraparound — defence: compiler >= 0.8 or checked math. Access-control failures: unprotected initialize() or admin functions. Front-running/MEV: transactions visible in the mempool are copyable (the Frontrunner's dilemma in AMM trades) — defences: commit-reveal, slippage bounds, private relays. Oracle manipulation: flash-loan-priced collateral attacks. Denial of service: unbounded loops, griefing via revert in pull-payment loops.",
            "Checks-effects-interactions is the pattern to name and illustrate for reentrancy. Wrong order: send ETH first, then set balances[msg.sender] = 0 — the fallback re-enters while the balance still shows funds. Right order: set the balance to zero (effects), then transfer (interaction), so re-entry sees an empty balance. Add the ReentrancyGuard modifier (a boolean mutex set before external calls) and the two defences compound. A second pattern, pull over push payments: never push ETH to arbitrary addresses inside loops (a revert from a malicious receiver bricks the whole loop); instead record credits and let recipients withdraw individually.",
