            "A token is a ledger entry managed by a contract rather than the protocol — a currency, point balance or asset whose rules live in Solidity. Fungibility means every unit is identical and interchangeable, like rupees; the ERC-20 standard (EIP-20, 2015) fixed the interface that made fungible tokens composable: every wallet, exchange and DApp can support any ERC-20 without reading its code, because the functions and events are guaranteed. The standard's nine functions and two events fit on one exam page: name, symbol, decimals (typically 18), totalSupply, balanceOf(address), transfer(to, amount), transferFrom(from, to, amount), approve(spender, amount), allowance(owner, spender), plus Transfer and Approval events.",
            "The approval mechanism is the standard's cleverest and most-misunderstood part, and tracing it is the exam staple. Direct flow: transfer(to, amount) moves the caller's own balance — msg.sender is the debit side. Delegated flow: the owner calls approve(spender, 100), storing allowances[owner][spender] = 100 and emitting Approval; later the spender calls transferFrom(owner, recipient, 30), which checks the allowance, debits it, and moves tokens — this is how DEXs and other contracts move user funds without users handing over keys. The UX cost: two transactions for any contract-mediated transfer (approve then swap), and the security footgun: unlimited approvals leave drainable allowances (mitigated by permit/EIP-2612 gasless approvals and approval-management dashboards).",
            "A complete minimal ERC-20 is the section's code artefact, and reproducing its skeleton demonstrates the whole standard: a mapping(address => uint256) balances, a mapping(address => mapping(address => uint256)) allowances, totalSupply minted in the constructor, transfer with require(balances[msg.sender] >= amount), approve and transferFrom with allowance accounting, and the two mandated events emitted on every state change. Production tokens inherit OpenZeppelin's audited implementation, adding mint/burn with access control, pausability, permit, and (controversially) blacklist or forced-transfer powers for regulated assets — the difference between the bare standard and the governance-bearing versions is itself an examinable point.",
            "ERC-20 economics and history round out the evaluation. The 2017 ICO wave used ERC-20 as the issuance rails — thousands of tokens launched, many as securities in retrospect, which shaped global regulation (Unit V). Variants answer specific complaints: ERC-777 adds hooks (with reentrancy-related pitfalls that forced an EIP migration), ERC-223 refunds failed transfers, ERC-2612/permit eliminates the approve transaction via signatures, and ERC-6909/404 explore partial fungibility. Known quirks to cite: tokens that do not return bool (USDT's non-standard transfer) break naive integrations — hence SafeERC20 wrappers; decimals ambiguity; and the balance-reading race during reentrancy. The standard is a lesson in interface power and interface debt at the same time.",
