            "Healthcare's pain is fragmented, siloed records with weak interoperability and audit gaps; blockchain's offer is a tamper-evident, patient-controlled access log rather than a replacement for medical databases. The sound architecture stores records off-chain (encrypted, in the hospital's system or IPFS) and puts on-chain only pointers plus hashes and an access-control event log: who was granted access, when, and to which hash. MedRec (MIT, 2016) is the canonical prototype; Estonia's KSI blockchain secures national health and government records' integrity. The privacy design point examiners reward: data never goes on the public ledger (GDPR erasure would be impossible); the chain guarantees record integrity and consent history, not confidentiality — that comes from encryption and access control.",
