          number: "5.8",
          title: "Case studies: Bitcoin, Ethereum, DeFi and failures",
          body: [
            "Bitcoin as a case study validates the core design and frames its trade-offs. Fourteen-plus years of continuous operation, no successful ledger forgery, survival of multiple exchange collapses and national bans, and a security budget that has made a majority attack economically irrational at scale — these are the strongest empirical results in the field. The same history exposes the design costs: throughput left to layers (Lightning's liquidity limits keep adoption niche), the long-run fee-funding question as subsidy decays, mining centralization by geography and energy price, and governance conservatism that leaves UX gaps (no native accounts, no recovery). The exam synthesis: Bitcoin proves trustless value transfer and censorship resistance are real and durable — and proves the price is a deliberately narrow function set.",
            "Ethereum's case study is one of managed evolution: ICO wave (2017) -> DAO hack and fork (2016, the founding governance precedent: code is law vs community intervention) -> DeFi summer (2020, composability proven) -> NFT wave (2021) -> the Merge (2022, a live consensus-layer migration without chain stop — an engineering landmark) -> EIP-1559 fee burn and rollup-centred scaling (2024 blobs). Each milestone is a teachable design decision: the fork established that social consensus overrides code when funds at scale are at risk; 1559 established protocol-captured value can be burned to align monetary policy; the Merge established that the hardest possible upgrade is executable with years of planning. The lesson sentence: Ethereum trades Bitcoin's immutability-of-design for mutability-with-coordination, and pays in governance complexity.",
            "The DeFi case studies are the application-layer evidence. Uniswap: an AMM that outcompeted order books for long-tail assets with one invariant (x*y=k) — composability made every frontend a Uniswap frontend; Impermanent Loss is its structural cost. MakerDAO/DAI: the first successful on-chain credit system, surviving the March 2020 crash via emergency mechanisms — and later the Maker->Sky rebrand showing DAO governance maturation. Aave/Compound: algorithmic interest rates from utilization curves. The counter-cases are equally instructive: Terra/UST (2022): an algorithmic stablecoin paying 20 percent yields collapsed, vaporizing ~$40B and ending the algorithmic-stablecoin category; FTX (2022): a centralized custodian misusing client funds — not a blockchain failure but the largest reputational shock, demonstrating that exchange custody recreates exactly the counterparty risk the technology removed.",
            "Enterprise and public-sector cases balance the retail-heavy narrative. IBM Food Trust and TradeLens: the two most-cited supply-chain chains; TradeLens's 2023 shutdown after failing to achieve industry-wide consortium economics is the essential negative result — technology readiness is not adoption. Estonia's KSI: hash-anchoring at national scale for a decade, the quiet success story of integrity anchoring without hype. Hyperledger Fabric deployments in trade finance and CBDC pilots (e-CNY's two-tier design, the e-rupee's token-based pilot) show permissioned rails entering production. India-specific cases for RGPV answers: state land-record pilots, the e-rupee, and NPCI-adjacent settlement experiments. The pattern across successes and failures: blockchain wins where a neutral shared record solves a real reconciliation pain among parties with leverage to join — and fails where a single motivated intermediary can do the job cheaper.",
            "The synthesis questions close the subject, and they are the exam's most probable 7-mark discussion prompts. Is blockchain trustless or trust-shifting? (It removes trusted intermediaries for record-keeping while introducing new trust in code correctness, key custody and governance.) Is decentralization a terminal value or an instrumental one? (Empirically instrumental: users accept L2 sequencers and stablecoin issuers when the product works.) Does immutability help or harm society? (Both: audit trails vs GDPR/bug permanence.) What did the last decade prove? (Trustless settlement works; general-purpose trustless computing is still being negotiated; adoption follows incentive design, not technology alone.) Preparing one paragraph per question, each anchored to a case study already covered, converts this section from trivia into the subject's conclusion — and it is exactly the shape of the final long-answer question on most papers.",
          ],
